---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../../components/Byline.astro";
import DualDoors from "../../components/DualDoors.astro";
import LegalNote from "../../components/LegalNote.astro";
import ThreeNumbersTable from "../../components/ThreeNumbersTable.astro";
import IndexSeriesTable from "../../components/IndexSeriesTable.astro";
import { FEATURED } from "../../lib/briefs";
import { LINKS, CANON_PATHS, linkClass, ext } from "../../lib/links";
import { newsArticleNode } from "../../lib/schema";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/base";

const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Notes", path: "/brief" },
  { name: FEATURED.title, path: FEATURED.path },
];

const pageUrl = "https://fflintel.com/brief/three-numbers";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${FEATURED.title} | FFL Intel`}
  description={FEATURED.dek}
  path={FEATURED.path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.copilot}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  extraJsonLd={[
    newsArticleNode({
      url: pageUrl,
      headline: FEATURED.title,
      description: FEATURED.dek,
      datePublished: FEATURED.date,
      dateModified: FEATURED.date,
      image: `https://fflintel.com/${CANON_PATHS.copilot}`,
    }),
  ]}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p
      class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
    >
      {FEATURED.kicker} · Print dated 13 April 2026
    </p>
    <h1
      class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-4xl text-balance mb-4"
    >
      {FEATURED.title}
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-300 mb-6">{FEATURED.dek}</p>
    <Byline dateLabel={FEATURED.dateLabel} read={FEATURED.read} />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.copilot)}
        alt={FEATURED.imageAlt}
        width="1792"
        height="1008"
        class="w-full object-cover object-top max-h-80"
      />
      <figcaption class="px-4 py-2 text-[11px] text-zinc-500">
        Research by Betsy. The signal is the print and the distinction — not
        the correspondent.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        On 13 April 2026 Outdoor Wire moved Gearfire’s RetailBI Firearm Sales
        Index for March. Same-store new firearm units, down 11.9 percent from
        March 2025, down 8.2 percent on the year. This desk does not sit in
        their Sisense. The file is public.
      </p>
      <p>
        A single monthly is weather. The public series is climate. August
        2025, the first monthly, printed minus 17 percent. November was the
        steep print: minus 22.2. The December file put 2025-over-2024 at minus
        12.1. The annual, later, called that climate about 13 percent. January
        2026 first printed minus 6.2 (Outdoor Wire, 9 February) and was
        revised to minus 5.7 on the February file (10 March), “to reflect the
        full same-store dataset.” February: minus 6.5, year-to-date minus 6.1.
        March steepened.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
      >
        The public prints
      </p>
      <IndexSeriesTable />
      <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] text-zinc-500">
        As of 13 August 2026 the last monthly on Outdoor Wire is the April 13
        file. This desk will not invent April through July.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <blockquote
        class="border-l-2 border-sky-500/60 pl-4 text-zinc-100 italic"
      >
        Figures are subject to revision as retailer data syncs are finalized.
        <span class="block mt-2 not-italic text-sm text-zinc-400"
          >— Outdoor Wire, 13 April 2026, RetailBI file</span
        >
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        That line is the method. A register panel is a sample. Their About on
        those files says more than two thousand U.S. firearms retailers. It is
        still a panel. It is still last month. It is still what rang.
      </p>
      <p>
        How they say they build it: point-of-sale and ecommerce from the
        network; new firearms only; same stores in both the current window and
        the year-ago window. Openings and closings do not pose as demand. They
        built the series because NICS, for decades, was a bad sales proxy —
        permits, used guns, transfers, denials, several guns on one check.
        Kaleb Seymour put that on Outdoor Wire on 6 June 2024. It is restated
        on every Index release. The argument is correct as far as the register
        goes.
      </p>
      <p>
        NSSF, 23 February 2026, carried Gearfire’s complimentary annual. 2025
        same-store new-gun units down about 13 percent from 2024. Their 2026
        outlook: another seven to twelve percent down, “in the absence of a
        meaningful demand catalyst.” Category color the monthly will not give:
        shotguns −16 percent, rifles −15.4, handguns −11.5 (still the
        highest-volume firearm category). Versus 2019, new firearms −10.5
        percent. Year-end inventory −10.6 percent. Ammo units −12.4. Optics
        down on the year; red dots more than doubled versus 2019. Suppressors
        −19.3 after a strong 2024; Gearfire attributes some of the drop to
        buyers waiting on the $200 tax stamp. Suppressor inventory +21.8
        percent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Used-gun color, labeled as such: Guns.com, Outdoor Wire 12 March 2026,
        claimed local-dealer sales on their platform up 46 percent in February
        and said they were outrunning that same 7–12 percent 2026 Index
        forecast. Pre-owned was about 31 percent of their mix. The Index is
        new firearms. Those sentences can all be true. Mixing them is how a
        forecast goes sideways.
      </p>
      <p>
        What the print cannot see is the ask — including a search that
        returned nothing. A zero-hit never becomes a same-store unit. It still
        happened. This desk will not invent a Betsy Live percentage and park
        it next to −11.9 as if it were a competing index. The distinction is
        the product of the research, not a rival monthly.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <p
        class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-sky-400 mb-3"
      >
        Three files. Do not staple them.
      </p>
      <ThreeNumbersTable />
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif"
    >
      <p>
        For brand teams already reading RetailBI, the long comparison of the
        two intelligence models lives on GunSearchEngine:{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.fortress} class={linkClass} {...ext}
          >RetailBI and Betsy, side by side</a
        >. For retailers, the on-site sensor is{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.gsa} class={linkClass} {...ext}>GunSearchAgent</a>
        — a different product than this note.
      </p>
    </div>

    <DualDoors heading="What to do with the distinction" />

    <p class="mt-10">
      <LegalNote />
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
